# R4 — strict-target (rejected: spec class R11)
#
# A valid strict certificate does not make its rule application defeasible.
# a1 reuses the audited premise via nd@1, exactly as S1 does. The reviewer
# supplies the policy's declared exception, but an undercut may target only
# a defeasible rule application: `undercut d1 a1.rule` is rejected with
# CE_R11 (StrictTarget certified_citation) at the typed-attack stage.
#
# The position exists and names a rule, so this is not R10. The exception
# matches, so this is not an unlicensed undercut either. No contrary is
# declared on the strict conclusion, so policy validation does not reject R12.

artifact strict_target at sha256:dddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddd
policy strict-target-v1
use backends [nd@1]

claim c1
  nl      = "The safety invariant holds for deployment D"
  formal  = holds(safety_invariant, D)
  binding = { author = alice, rationale = "Certified reuse of the audited safety invariant.", audit-status = reviewed }

leaf e1 : holds(safety_invariant, D)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [evidence/safety_audit.txt#section=invariants]

arg a1 : supports(c1) by certified_citation from [e1]
  assurance = cert(nd@1, sha256:strict-target-v1-theory-0, (hyp 0))

# The source of the attempted undercut is a valid leaf-rooted argument.
claim c_review
  nl      = "The safety audit for deployment D is challenged"
  formal  = audit_challenged(safety_invariant, D)
  binding = { author = bob, rationale = "The review disputes the audit's evidence, not certificate replay.", audit-status = reviewed }

leaf e2 : audit_challenged(safety_invariant, D)
  kind       = attested
  provenance = user
  refs       = [evidence/review.txt#section=audit]

arg d1 : supports(c_review) by leaf(e2)

# ILLEGAL: an exception declaration cannot authorize an undercut on a strict step.
undercut d1 a1.rule

status c1

# EXPECTED VERDICT — verified by test/WorkedExamplesSpec.hs:
# reject R11, exit 1; located diagnostic: typed-attack, attack index 0,
# `undercut d1 a1` (root position; source spelling: `a1.rule`).
# Removing this attack leaves a justified claim.
#
# Teaching point: strict protects the inference, not the truth of its inputs.
# A review disputing the attested premise must use a policy-licensed undermine
# of that leaf (as S4 disputes a binding leaf), not an undercut of the strict step.
